# Technical Erratum & Formal Boundary Specification: Two-Gate Discrete Solvency

**Classification:** Engineering specification · formal invariant record  
**Target architecture:** CH4R0N / DR4G0N pre-dispatch execution pipeline  
**Standard of proof:** Finite-dimensional linear algebra (Gate A) and componentwise order on \(\mathbb{N}^4\) (Gate B)  
**Status:** Locked text with displayed identities restored

---

## 1. Mechanically proven invariants

The system boundary is two deterministic, single-step execution gates.

### Gate A — Single-step linear Poisson residual closure

Let \(V=\mathbb{R}^n\) with the standard inner product.  
Let \(L_w\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\) be symmetric positive semi-definite (weighted graph Laplacian).  
Then \(\operatorname{ran}(L_w)=\ker(L_w)^\perp\).  
Let \(L_w^+\) denote the unique Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse, and let

\[
P_{\ker(L_w)}\qquad\text{and}\qquad
P_{\operatorname{ran}(L_w)}=I-P_{\ker(L_w)}
\]

be the orthogonal projectors onto \(\ker(L_w)\) and \(\operatorname{ran}(L_w)\).

**Lemma A.1 (range invariance).**  
For any discrete load \(b_\theta\in\mathbb{R}^n\),

\[
b_\theta \perp \ker(L_w)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
b_\theta\in\operatorname{ran}(L_w)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
P_{\ker(L_w)} b_\theta = 0.
\]

**Theorem A.1 (exact Moore–Penrose residual identity).**  
Let \(\Phi^*:=L_w^+ b_\theta\). Then, for every \(b_\theta\in\mathbb{R}^n\),

\begin{equation}
L_w\Phi^*
= L_w L_w^+ b_\theta
= P_{\operatorname{ran}(L_w)} b_\theta
= b_\theta - P_{\ker(L_w)} b_\theta.
\tag{A.1}
\end{equation}

The linear residual of the discrete Poisson problem is therefore

\begin{equation}
R_A
:= L_w\Phi^* - b_\theta
= -\,P_{\ker(L_w)} b_\theta.
\tag{A.2}
\end{equation}

Under the range condition of Lemma A.1,

\begin{equation}
b_\theta\perp\ker(L_w)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
P_{\ker(L_w)} b_\theta=0
\;\Longrightarrow\;
L_w\Phi^*=b_\theta
\;\Longrightarrow\;
R_A=0.
\tag{A.3}
\end{equation}

If the range condition fails,

\begin{equation}
R_A = -\,P_{\ker(L_w)} b_\theta \neq 0,
\tag{A.4}
\end{equation}

and any claim that \(R_A=0\) is refused.

**Formal boundary.** Exact zero residual is guaranteed exclusively for the discrete linear system \(L_w\Phi=b_\theta\) at a single time step, and only when \(b_\theta\perp\ker(L_w)\).

---

### Gate B — Deterministic monotonic resource solvency

Let the interior at this tick be

\[
I=\begin{bmatrix} I_C\\ I_M\\ I_P\\ I_Q\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{N}^4.
\]

(\(I\) is the measured capacity at the tick, not an eternal constant.)  
Let \(v_{\mathrm{active}}\in\mathbb{N}^4\) be the already-allocated state and \(v_{\mathrm{req}}\in\mathbb{N}^4\) the prospective increment.

**Definition B.1 (componentwise partial order).**  
For \(x,y\in\mathbb{N}^4\),

\begin{equation}
x\le y
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\bigwedge_{i=1}^{4}(x_i\le y_i).
\tag{B.1}
\end{equation}

**Definition B.2 (checked addition).**  
Ordinary addition of naturals does not wrap. A 128-bit implementation may still overflow a machine word; that overflow is identified with refusal:

\begin{equation}
a\oplus c
=
\begin{cases}
a+c & \text{if }a+c<2^{128},\\
\bot & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\tag{B.2}
\end{equation}

Componentwise \(\oplus\) on \(\mathbb{N}^4\) returns \(\bot\) if any limb does.

**Definition B.3 (admission map).**  
Let \(\tau\in\{\mathrm{TRUE},\mathrm{FALSE}\}\) be the telemetry-valid flag (residual vector and capacity reading received). The prospective allocation is \(v_{\mathrm{prop}}:=v_{\mathrm{active}}+v_{\mathrm{req}}\) when that sum is defined. The transition

\[
\mathcal{T}:
\mathbb{N}^4\times\mathbb{N}^4\times\mathbb{N}^4\times\{\mathrm{TRUE},\mathrm{FALSE}\}
\to
\{\mathrm{COMMIT\_OK},\,\mathrm{REFUSE\_CAPACITY},\,\mathrm{REFUSE\_TELEMETRY}\}
\]

is

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}(v_{\mathrm{active}},v_{\mathrm{req}},I,\tau)
=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{REFUSE\_TELEMETRY}
  & \text{if }\tau=\mathrm{FALSE},\\[4pt]
\mathrm{REFUSE\_CAPACITY}
  & \text{if }\tau=\mathrm{TRUE}
    \text{ and }
    \bigl(v_{\mathrm{active}}\oplus v_{\mathrm{req}}=\bot
    \ \vee\
    v_{\mathrm{active}}+v_{\mathrm{req}}\not\le I\bigr),\\[4pt]
\mathrm{COMMIT\_OK}
  & \text{if }\tau=\mathrm{TRUE}
    \text{ and }
    v_{\mathrm{active}}\oplus v_{\mathrm{req}}\ne\bot
    \text{ and }
    v_{\mathrm{active}}+v_{\mathrm{req}}\le I.
\end{cases}
\tag{B.3}
\end{equation}

**Theorem B.1 (fail-closed safety on gated branches).**  
On every branch that is required to call \(\mathcal{T}\) before mutation,

\begin{equation}
v_{\mathrm{active}}
\;\leftarrow\;
\begin{cases}
v_{\mathrm{active}}+v_{\mathrm{req}}
  & \text{if }\mathcal{T}=\mathrm{COMMIT\_OK},\\
v_{\mathrm{active}}
  & \text{otherwise,}
\end{cases}
\tag{B.4}
\end{equation}

equivalently \(\Delta v_{\mathrm{active}}=0\) unless \(\mathcal{T}=\mathrm{COMMIT\_OK}\).  
Hence no gated branch commits a state with \(v\not\le I\).

This is an invariant of branches that pass through \(\mathcal{T}\). It is not a theorem about ungated code paths.

**Formal boundary.** Hardware capacity bounds cannot be exceeded on any execution branch that is routed through \(\mathcal{T}\).

---

### Coupled discrete step

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{charonAdmit}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\mathcal{T}=\mathrm{COMMIT\_OK}
\;\wedge\;
\bigl(R_A=0\ \text{or zero-residual is not claimed}\bigr).
\tag{C.1}
\end{equation}

A zero-residual claim additionally requires \(b_\theta\perp\ker(L_w)\), i.e. (A.3).

