## 2. Explicit non-equivalence (non-claims)

| Discrete identity | is not |
|---|---|
| \(L_w(L_w^+ b_\theta)=b_\theta\) when \(b_\theta\perp\ker(L_w)\) | continuum Navier–Stokes regularity \(\partial_t u+(u\cdot\nabla)u=-\nabla p+\nu\Delta u\) |
| Kuramoto phase lock \(\dot\phi_i=\Omega_i+K\sum_j\sin(\phi_j-\phi_i)\) | vortex stretching \((\omega\cdot\nabla)u\) |
| \(v\le I\) on a 128-bit word | a fluid topological invariant (e.g. helicity \(\int u\cdot\omega\,dV\), which this spec does not conserve) |

- Graph projection of continuum NS is an approximation. \(R_A=0\) does not bound \(\|\omega\|_\infty\) and does not imply \(\int_0^T\|\omega(\cdot,t)\|_\infty\,dt<\infty\).  
- Lyapunov descent on \(\mathbb{T}^n\) is oscillator consensus, not an energy cascade or turbulence suppression theorem.  
- \(v\le I\) is a pre-dispatch guard, not a continuum conservation law.

---

## 3. Signal flow

```
[ physical / sensor domain ]
              │  discretization & modeling approximation
              ▼
[ discrete graph G = (V, E) ]
              │
     ┌────────┴────────┐
     ▼                 ▼
 Gate A             Gate B
 b_θ ⊥ ker(L_w)     T(v_active, v_req, I, τ)
 Φ* = L_w⁺ b_θ      COMMIT_OK / REFUSE_*
 R_A = L_w Φ* − b_θ
     │                 │
     └────────┬────────┘
              ▼
 [ admissible discrete step committed ]
 exact single-step discrete closure;
 no continuum regularity implied
```

---

## 4. Canonical record

Exact single-step linear residual closure is guaranteed when the load lies in \(\ker(L_w)^\perp\). Fail-closed resource solvency is guaranteed on branches that invoke \(\mathcal{T}\) before mutation. These assertions are algebraic and discrete. They do not establish continuum fluid regularity, global Navier–Stokes smoothness, or turbulence suppression.
